(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-ii-vignettes"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies II Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has excisional biopsy-proven diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Computed tomography and positron emission tomography show disease confined to one group of right cervical lymph nodes, with no other nodes or organs involved. What is her stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lugano staging, a modified Ann Arbor system: stage 1 is localized disease in a single lymph node region or a single organ. One right cervical node region with nothing else involved is stage 1. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma with involved lymph nodes in the neck and the groin and no extranodal sites. His ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance status is 1, and his lactate dehydrogenase is above the upper limit of normal. How many poor-prognosis factors does he have?","choices":["One factor","Two factors","Three factors","Four factors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Item by item from the poor-prognosis list: age over 60, yes (67); extranodal sites, no; ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) status above 2, no (1); stage III or IV, yes (neck and groin nodes span the diaphragm, stage III); elevated lactate dehydrogenase, yes. Total 3. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 26, 30"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with splenomegaly is found to have splenic marginal zone lymphoma. Which associated infection should be tested for?","choices":["Hepatitis C virus","Helicobacter pylori","Epstein-Barr virus","Lyme disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Splenic marginal zone lymphoma is associated with hepatitis C; marginal zone lymphomas are more common in older adults. Her splenic subtype points to hepatitis C rather than Helicobacter pylori. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 17, 23"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has newly diagnosed Burkitt lymphoma, a high-grade lymphoma. Excisional biopsy, imaging, blood counts, chemistries and lactate dehydrogenase have been done. Which additional procedure is required in his work-up?","choices":["Upper endoscopy","Protein electrophoresis","Lumbar puncture","Fine-needle aspiration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-grade lymphomas, including Burkitt lymphoma, require a lumbar puncture as part of the work-up, alongside bone marrow biopsy, imaging, a complete blood count, chemistries and lactate dehydrogenase. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has smoldering multiple myeloma: M-protein 3.6 g/dL and 18% clonal bone marrow plasma cells, with no anemia, bone lesions, renal failure or hypercalcemia. No high-risk subgroup feature is identified. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Monitor without treatment","Start a 3-drug regimen now","Autologous stem cell transplant","Allopurinol and fluids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Smoldering myeloma typically does not require treatment, although early intervention may be considered for some high-risk subgroups; she has none identified. It progresses at about 10% per year, so monitoring continues. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has back pain and constipation. His M-protein is 4.6 g/dL and bone marrow biopsy shows 38% clonal plasma cells. Calcium is 12.1 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL), hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), creatinine 2.0 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2 mg/dL), and imaging shows lytic lesions in the spine. How should his plasma cell disorder be classified?","choices":["Multiple myeloma","Smoldering multiple myeloma","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance","Reactive hypergammaglobulinemia of cirrhosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myeloma is diagnosed by at least 10% marrow plasma cells (38%) plus at least one CRAB (calcium, renal, anemia, bone) feature; he has all four: hypercalcemia (12.1), renal insufficiency (creatinine 2.0), anemia (9.2) and lytic bone lesions. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 47, 50, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with chronic lymphocytic leukemia confirmed by flow cytometry has easy bruising. His absolute lymphocyte count is 88,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000). He has no palpable lymph nodes and no hepatomegaly or splenomegaly. Hemoglobin is 13.9 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) and platelets 64,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000). What is his Rai stage?","choices":["Stage 0","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Item by item: lymphocytosis yes (88,000); lymphadenopathy no; organomegaly no; anemia no (13.9 g/dL); thrombocytopenia yes (64,000, with easy bruising). Lymphocytosis with thrombocytopenia is stage IV with or without nodes, organomegaly or anemia. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man has classical Hodgkin lymphoma with cervical nodes and a bulky mediastinal mass, all above the diaphragm, and drenching night sweats. Which treatment approach is appropriate?","choices":["Observation","Chemotherapy plus radiation","A short course of chemotherapy","Radiation alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His nodes are on one side of the diaphragm (stage II), and the disease is bulky with B symptoms (night sweats). Bulky stage I or II disease, or disease with B symptoms, is treated with chemotherapy plus radiation. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 16, 27, 41"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman has just been told she has chronic lymphocytic leukemia, Rai stage 0. She asks what her long-term outlook is. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["She is likely to die of it in 2 years","It is cured by a short chemotherapy course","It turns into aggressive lymphoma in a year","She is likely to die with it, not of it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Five-year relative survival is about 88%, much improved by novel agents, and the elderly age at onset with ongoing new therapies means many patients will die with chronic lymphocytic leukemia rather than from it. Long-term prognosis is likely very good. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a non-aggressive (low-grade) B-cell lymphoma confined to a single lymph node region in the left groin; staging shows no other disease. Which treatment fits this presentation?","choices":["Allogeneic transplant","Radiation therapy","Multi-drug chemotherapy","Ibrutinib"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Management depends on subtype and presentation: a single node of non-aggressive disease is treated with radiation. His staging shows one region only (stage 1) of low-grade disease. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 27, 11"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has chronic lymphocytic leukemia confirmed by flow cytometry. She asks whether any other test will help predict her course and guide her therapy. Which test serves that purpose?","choices":["Positron emission tomography","Urine protein electrophoresis","Cytogenetics","Bone marrow biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cytogenetics is important for prognosis and has some treatment considerations. About 80% of cases carry one of four chromosomal abnormalities: deletion 13q, deletion 11q, trisomy 12 or deletion 17p. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 5, 8"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance: M-protein 1.1 g/dL, 3% clonal bone marrow plasma cells and no myeloma-defining characteristics. She asks how likely it is to become myeloma. Which rate is accurate?","choices":["About 1% per year","About 10% per year","About 25% per year","About 50% per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The risk of progression from monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance to myeloma is about 1% per year. Her low M-protein, under 10% plasma cells and lack of myeloma-defining features keep her in this low-risk precursor stage. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man's primary care clinician finds hypercalcemia, anemia and an M-protein, and bone marrow biopsy confirms multiple myeloma. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Nephrology for kidney evaluation alone","Orthopedic surgery for the bone lesions","Endocrinology for the high calcium","Hematology/oncology, ideally a myeloma expert"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Multiple myeloma requires referral to and management by a hematologist/oncologist, ideally a myeloma expert, with lifelong follow-up for lab monitoring and treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has newly diagnosed multiple myeloma. His beta-2 microglobulin is 6.4 mg/L (normal 0.8-2.2 mg/L) and albumin 3.8 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL); chromosomal studies show only standard-risk abnormalities, and lactate dehydrogenase is normal. What is his R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage III needs all of beta-2 microglobulin at least 5.5 mg/L, high-risk chromosomes and high lactate dehydrogenase; he has only the high beta-2 microglobulin. Stage I needs beta-2 microglobulin below 3.5 mg/L, and his is 6.4. Not stage I or III is stage II. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with chronic dyspepsia has an endoscopic gastric biopsy showing extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue. Which associated infection should be tested for?","choices":["Helicobacter pylori","Hepatitis C virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Human T-lymphotropic virus 1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma, the most common extranodal marginal zone subtype, is associated with Helicobacter pylori in about two-thirds of cases. A stomach lymphoma of this type calls for testing for it. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 17, 23"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has an absolute lymphocyte count of 4,600 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) on routine blood work. Flow cytometry shows a clonal B-cell population. She has no symptoms, no lymphadenopathy and no hepatomegaly or splenomegaly; hemoglobin and platelets are normal. How should this finding be classified?","choices":["Monoclonal B lymphocytosis","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Infectious atypical lymphocytosis","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her absolute lymphocyte count is elevated but below 5,000 per microliter, and she has no lymphadenopathy, organomegaly, cytopenias or symptoms. That is monoclonal B lymphocytosis, which progresses to chronic lymphocytic leukemia at about 1-2% per year. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had chronic lymphocytic leukemia, observed without treatment, for 6 years. Over 3 weeks one cervical lymph node has grown rapidly, and he now has fevers, night sweats and weight loss. Which complication has most likely occurred?","choices":["Smoldering myeloma","Richter transformation","Tumor lysis syndrome","Monoclonal B lymphocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Richter transformation is chronic lymphocytic leukemia transforming into an aggressive B-cell lymphoma, with poor outcomes. A long, stable course that suddenly turns into a rapidly growing node with B symptoms is that picture. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 28, 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has painless, firm, mobile lymph nodes in the neck and both axillae. His absolute lymphocyte count is 4,500 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000), and flow cytometry shows a clonal B-cell population coexpressing CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5). How should this be classified?","choices":["Infectious atypical lymphocytosis","Classical Hodgkin lymphoma","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Monoclonal B lymphocytosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is diagnosed with a count above 5,000 for 3 months or a count below 5,000 with lymphadenopathy or other symptoms. His count is 4,500, but he has lymphadenopathy, and the clone coexpresses CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5). — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has a painless, rubbery left cervical lymph node that aches after she drinks alcohol, and intense itching for 2 months. Chest x-ray shows a mediastinal mass. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Burkitt lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma","Hodgkin lymphoma","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma has a bimodal incidence with a peak in adults in their 20s and presents with painless lymphadenopathy, usually cervical, that may hurt with alcohol, intense pruritus and a mediastinal mass on chest x-ray. Her picture matches item by item. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 37, 38"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Staging shows enlarged cervical lymph nodes, lymphoma deposits in the liver, and bone marrow biopsy involvement. What is her stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoma in the liver and the bone marrow is widespread disease with multiple organs involved, which is stage 4 with or without lymph node involvement. Her cervical nodes do not change that. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has cutaneous T-cell lymphoma that has progressed to Sezary syndrome: a widespread, red, itchy rash (erythroderma) with severe pruritus. Staging finds no visceral involvement. What is the focus of his treatment?","choices":["Splenectomy","Treating the skin disease","Rituximab-based chemotherapy","Antifungal therapy alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (mycosis fungoides and its aggressive form, Sezary syndrome) is focused on the cutaneous manifestations, as there is often no visceral involvement. His erythroderma and pruritus diminish his quality of life. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 22, 27"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with sporadic Burkitt lymphoma is about to begin multi-agent chemotherapy. His potassium, phosphorus, uric acid and creatinine are currently normal. What should be done before the first dose?","choices":["Delay chemotherapy for 2 weeks","Give allopurinol and intravenous fluids","Restrict fluids to protect the kidneys","Give oral potassium supplements"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome should be anticipated in aggressive lymphoma, and Burkitt lymphoma needs caution when therapy starts. Patients are given allopurinol (a hypouricemic agent) and fluids in advance, with aggressive intravenous hydration. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 28, 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has newly diagnosed multiple myeloma. His beta-2 microglobulin is 2.9 mg/L (normal 0.8-2.2 mg/L), albumin 4.0 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL), chromosomal studies show only standard-risk abnormalities, and lactate dehydrogenase is normal. What is his R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage I requires every item: beta-2 microglobulin below 3.5 mg/L (2.9), albumin at least 3.5 g/dL (4.0), standard-risk chromosomal abnormalities, and normal lactate dehydrogenase. He meets all four. The stage is prognostic and does not broadly change treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a painless, persistent 3-cm cervical lymph node, and Hodgkin lymphoma is suspected. The team is planning how to sample it. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Complete blood count","Fine-needle aspiration","Excisional lymph node biopsy","Chest x-ray"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fine-needle aspiration should be avoided: excisional biopsy is required to observe lymph node architecture, and aspiration may miss the diagnosis. Hodgkin lymphoma is diagnosed by excisional lymph node biopsy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has chronic lymphocytic leukemia, Rai stage 0, found on routine blood work. He has no symptoms, and his absolute lymphocyte count of 12,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) has been stable for 9 months. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Venetoclax plus rituximab","Excision of the nodes","Observation (watch and wait)","Start ibrutinib now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic patients may be observed (watch and wait) until there is rapid progression (lymphocyte count doubling in under 6 months) or disease-related symptoms. Treatment begins with symptomatic disease or rapid progression; he has neither. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 10, 11"},{"q":"A 71-year-old farmer with newly diagnosed chronic lymphocytic leukemia asks whether the pesticides he sprayed for decades caused his leukemia. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Pesticides likely caused it","Hepatitis C caused it","Radiation likely caused it","No exposure is a known risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia has no known occupational or environmental risk factors. Its risk factors are age (median onset 70), male sex (twice as common), and Eastern European or Jewish descent. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man was treated with chemotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma at age 25 and has been in remission since. He now has new cytopenias, and marrow evaluation shows a myelodysplastic syndrome. How is this best explained?","choices":["A relapse of Hodgkin lymphoma","Richter transformation","His inherited sibling risk","A delayed toxicity of his therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is generally curable (more than 90% alive at 5 years), but survivors may have delayed toxicities, including myelodysplastic syndrome, secondary leukemia and other cancers. Two decades after chemotherapy, his new marrow disease fits. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with Burkitt lymphoma started multi-agent chemotherapy 36 hours ago. His potassium is 6.6 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0 mEq/L), phosphorus 7.8 mg/dL (normal 2.5-4.5 mg/dL), uric acid 13.1 mg/dL (normal 3.5-7.2 mg/dL) and creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2 mg/dL). Which complication has developed?","choices":["Richter transformation","Hypercalcemia of myeloma","IgM hyperviscosity","Tumor lysis syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome is rapid tumor breakdown releasing potassium, phosphorus and uric acid; uric acid and calcium phosphate crystals in the renal tubules cause acute renal failure. It is a medical emergency, expected after treatment of aggressive lymphoma such as Burkitt. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Staging imaging shows enlarged right cervical lymph nodes and left inguinal lymph nodes, with no liver, bone marrow or other organ involvement. What is his stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cervical nodes sit above the diaphragm and inguinal nodes below it, with no organ involvement. Two or more lymph node regions above and below the diaphragm is stage 3. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has an absolute lymphocyte count of 18,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) on two blood counts 3 months apart. She feels well, with no lymphadenopathy or organomegaly. Which test confirms the suspected diagnosis?","choices":["Protein electrophoresis","Flow cytometry","Bone marrow biopsy","Excisional node biopsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her persistent lymphocytosis above 5,000 suggests chronic lymphocytic leukemia, which is confirmed by flow cytometry identifying a clonal B-cell population (coexpressing CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5)). It usually does not need a bone marrow biopsy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
